      "verdict": "MATTERS",
      "why": "The zone-index compatibility shim lets in more than zod 3 ever did. zod 3's z.string().ip() accepted the zoned address fe80::1%eth0 but rejected 2459::52d2%lo0, 2001:db8::ff00:42:8329%wlan0 and ::%1. The merged build accepts hosts [\"2459::52d2%lo0\"], which the zod 3 oracle rejects at hosts.0. The merged build also accepts zone text that contains a space, shell metacharacters or path traversal (::1%a b, ::1%$(reboot), 2001:db8::1%../../etc/passwd: 3 of 3 accepted by main.parseConfig, 0 of 3 by the oracle). Judgement: this is a genuine widening of accepted input, not a restoration, and it contradicts the premise the earlier sign-off rested on (review/corpus-diffs.md argues the union 'reproduces exactly the set zod 3 matched' so 'nothing new is let in'). Judgement: hosts values are the ones most likely to be handed to connect/DNS/shell-adjacent code, so an unconstrained zone suffix is the risky end of the widening; a human should narrow zonedIpv6 (keep zod 3's link-local-with-zone grammar and restrict the zone to the characters zod 3 allowed) before this ships.",
